Phase 1: Generate your GitHub Access Token
	Before using Eclipse, you must create a token to log in securely
	Log into your account on GitHub
	Go to Settings > Developer Settings > Personal Access Tokens > Tokens (classic)
	Click Generate new token
	Give it a name
	Check the box for the repo scope (to manage repositories)
	Click Generate token
	Copy the token immediately
	GitHub will not show it to you again

Workflow A: Push an Existing Eclipse Project to GitHub
	Create a Blank Repository on GitHub
	Go to GitHub and create a New Repository
	Give it a name, leave it blank (do not add a README, .gitignore, or license)
	Click Create repository
	Copy the HTTPS URL of the repository (e.g., https://github.com)

2. Initialize a Local Git Repository in Eclipse
	Inside Eclipse, right-click your project name in the Project Explorer
	Select Team > Share Project....Choose Git and click Next
	In "Repository" line, select or create and select where the respository folder will be saved
	click on your project name
	click Create Repository
	click Finish
	
3. Commit Your Files
	Right-click your project
	Select Team > Commit....This opens the Git Staging view.
	Select the files you want to track from Unstaged Changes
	click the + sign (or drag them) to move them into Staged Changes.
	Type a Commit Message (e.g., "Initial commit")
	click Commit and Push
	
	An error will pop up - this is okay

4. Authenticate and Push to GitHub
	In Git Staging window, select "Push HEAD"
	In the Destination window, paste the GitHub HTTPS URL you copied earlier into the URI field.
	Under Authentication
		User: Your GitHub username
		Password: Your Personal Access Token (not your GitHub password)
	Click Next
	Choose your branch (usually main)
	Click Next again
	Click Finish

Your code is now live on GitHub.